## 1. Method and scope

For every consequential [F]-labeled claim in the proposal I located the cited capture, re-read the exact body, and compared the claim's wording, numbers, dates, and locators against the capture text. I additionally ran four small executable checks in the admitted isolated sandbox (§5) and performed one fresh live re-fetch of the most time-sensitive volatile claim (§4, V-1). I did not re-derive the design sections ([C]/[I]) as "true or false"; I audited them for obligation coverage (§6) and internal consistency.

Reading coverage: I opened every source capture except four small auxiliary files I verified through the index and catalog only (see §3, note N5). The DuckDB issue→fix→regression-test chain, both tag pins, the nbformat schema + directory listing, marimo docs/search, papermill README, PEP 723, both Python-version sources, and the concurrency doc were read in full or in the sections the claims rely on.

**Overall verdict:** the research is substantially source-correct. Every load-bearing claim I checked traces to real capture content, including the two I initially suspected were overclaims (the S3 "Quack protocol" claim and the S13 "buffer-pool code visible in the tag commit" claim — both are literally present in their captures). I found **no substantive factual correction to the proposal text**; corrections are confined to catalog locators/metadata and two small spec gaps. Key claims are preserved with evidence below.

