## 3. Corrections (evidence-backed; none change the design conclusions)

**CR1 (catalog locator defect — S21 item indices swapped). Severity: low, must fix in the final catalog.**
`sources.json` S21 locator says "items[4] #10912 … items[3] #10915". Actual capture order in `0010.body` is items[0]=#10602, items[1]=#3140, items[2]=#9881, **items[3]=#10916** (an unrelated merged-then-reverted-status PR "fix: clean up stale UI element on re-registration"), **items[4]=#10915, items[5]=#10912**. Executed check C2 asserts this (exit 0, exec-e32tbk3y). The substantive facts attached to #10912/#10915 (dates, merge status, generation-counter content) are all correct — only the indices are wrong. **Affected dependency:** none on the proposal text (it cites [S21] without indices); fix the catalog line. A fresh author must not "correct" the #10912/#10915 facts — only the indices.

**CR2 (catalog pin parentheticals wrong for S20/S21). Severity: low.**
Both entries claim "first 32768 bytes captured". Actual captures are complete: `0009.body` is 393,173 bytes and `0010.body` is 44,688 bytes; the index sha256s match the **full** bodies (verified by the read tool's reported byte counts and the shared sha256 values). The parentheticals appear to be stale notes from an earlier partial-fetch attempt. Fix: delete or correct them; the sha256 pins themselves are valid. Also note `0009`'s index metadata says `truncated: false` while S20's note implies truncation — the index is right.

**CR3 (catalog capture identity unverifiable). Severity: low.**
`sources.json` `capture_id` values are 32-hex strings (e.g. S2 `9d162850…`) that match neither the source index sha256 (`12897abe…`) nor any prefix of it. They are presumably an internal digest I cannot recompute. The catalog contract requires "capture identity"; recommend the final catalog carry the **full sha256 of the capture body** (as the index already does) so a fresh reader can verify identity. No claim depends on the old values.

**CR4 (date-precision nuance on the 3.10 EOL date). Severity: informational.**
endoflife.date gives 3.10 EOL = 2026-10-01 with day precision; the devguide/PEP 619 schedule gives "October 2026". The proposal's conclusion ("already past" at capture; floor 3.11) is unaffected and my check C4 confirms it against the capture, but the final proposal should attribute day precision to the aggregator, not to python.org. **Affected dependency:** §11 wording only.

**CR5 (spec gap: cancel/interrupt of a *clean replay*). Severity: low, close it in the final.**
§6 defines cancel semantics for interactive runs (running cell + queued descendants → `unverified`) and §7 says clean replay has "progress, cancel", but no line states what happens to the partially-written replay artifact and its status overlay when a replay is cancelled or dies mid-run. By the design's own rules it should be: artifact kept, all its outputs `unverified`, event log records an interrupted event (never `current`). One sentence fixes it. **Affected dependency:** §7/§10 recovery text; W3's state machine already implements the needed transition.

**N1 (note, not a defect).** One capture claim needed care and survived: S13's locator ("file list in the tagged commit's parent diff shows … buffer_pool.cpp EvictBlocksInternal/SetLimit") is literally true because the GitHub commit endpoint returned per-file patches for the tag commit; see §2. Similarly S3's "Quack remote protocol beta as of v1.5.2" is verbatim in the capture.

**N2 (note).** The proposal's §2.1 phrase "an `outputs` array restricted to exactly four output types" is accurate for the v4.5 `output` oneOf; the schema's separate `unrecognized_output` definition exists but is not part of that oneOf. No change needed.

**N3 (note).** PR #12679 has `"body": null` — the proposal never attributes content to the PR body (it correctly uses the issue body and the diff), so nothing to fix; recorded so a future author doesn't expect a PR description.

**N4 (note).** `0007.body` and `0016.body` are byte-identical (same sha256) for two superficially different search query strings (`12596 in:body type:pr` vs `type:pr 12596`) — GitHub normalizes token order. Not a defect; explains the duplicate.

**N5 (note, catalog completeness).** Four index captures have no catalog entry: `0001` (unpinned CSV overview), `0007`/`0016` (the #12596 PR searches), `0019` (devguide root listing). I checked: **none of them backs any proposal claim**; they are discovery-trail artifacts. Either add them as uncited discovery captures or state in the catalog that index files without S-ids are intermediate fetches.

